      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Parajanov's defiance of Soviet realism cost him dearly - he was imprisoned from 1973 to 1977 on politically motivated charges. Yet his visual style, drawing from Armenian miniature paintings and medieval iconography, created a new cinematic vocabulary that influenced directors like Martin Scorsese and Mikhail Vartanov. **Shadows of Forgotten Ancestors** (1965), his earlier breakthrough, shows similar rejection of socialist realism for folk-inspired mysticism."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's staging techniques revolutionized art house cinema. Each composition draws from Armenian manuscript illuminations, creating living paintings that echo through later works like **The Falls** (1980) and **Institute Benjamenta** (1995) by the Brothers Quay. The ritualistic movements and symbolic color palette would later influence Tarsem Singh's **The Fall** (2006) and **The Cell** (2000). SUBHEAD: Impact on Modern Experimental Cinema"
      },
      {
